{"id":"burnt-offering","name":"Burnt Offering","aliases":["Burnt Offering"],"sources":["nave","torrey","easton"],"topics":["nave:burnt-offering","torrey:burnt-offering-the"],"groups":[],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Burnt offering","text":"Hebrew olah; i.e., “ascending,” the whole being consumed by fire, and regarded as ascending to God while being consumed. Part of every offering was burnt in the sacred fire, but this was wholly burnt, a “whole burnt offering.” It was the most frequent form of sacrifice, and apparently the only one mentioned in the book of Genesis. Such were the sacrifices offered by Abel (Gen. 4:3, 4, here called minhah; i.e., “a gift”), Noah (Gen. 8:20), Abraham (Gen. 22:2, 7, 8, 13), and by the Hebrews in Egypt (Ex. 10:25). The law of Moses afterwards prescribed the occasions and the manner in which burnt sacrifices were to be offered. There were “the continual burnt offering” (Ex. 29:38-42; Lev. 6:9-13), “the burnt offering of every sabbath,” which was double the daily one (Num. 28:9, 10), “the burnt offering of every month” (28:11-15), the offerings at the Passover (19-23), at Pentecost (Lev. …"},"lemmas":[{"strong":"H5930","lemma":"עֹלָה","xlit":"ʻôlâh","gloss":"a step or","kjv":"ascent, burnt offering, go up to. See also H5766","count":69,"share":0.55},{"strong":"H4196","lemma":"מִזְבֵּחַ","xlit":"mizbêach","gloss":"an altar","kjv":"altar","count":22,"share":0.18},{"strong":"H2077","lemma":"זֶבַח","xlit":"zebach","gloss":"a slaughter","kjv":"offer, sacrifice","count":17,"share":0.14},{"strong":"H85","lemma":"אַבְרָהָם","xlit":"ʼAbrâhâm","gloss":"Abraham, the later name of Abram","kjv":"Abraham","count":16,"share":0.13},{"strong":"H3532","lemma":"כֶּבֶשׂ","xlit":"kebes","gloss":"a ram","kjv":"lamb, sheep","count":14,"share":0.11},{"strong":"H7126","lemma":"קָרַב","xlit":"qârab","gloss":"to approach for whatever purpose","kjv":"approach, bring, come, draw near","count":15,"share":0.12}],"verses":["Gen.8.20","Gen.22.2","Gen.22.3","Gen